course: dictionary
lesson: 4655
title: One small word, many jobs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ho is a tiny word, but it can do different jobs. It is an interjection, a short word that bursts out to get attention or show a feeling. Its meaning depends on the words around it, so use context to decide what it means.
explain: Sailors shouted «Land ho!» when they spotted land. It means «land ahead!». The word after ho tells you what the sailor sees, so the sentence gives you the clue.
explain: Santa's laugh is «Ho ho ho!». Here the repeated ho is a laugh, not a shout. «Westward ho!» means «go west!». The same sound does different jobs, so always check the context.
explain: «Gung ho» came into English in the 1940s from a Chinese slogan, «gōnghé», understood as «work together». Now it means very eager, sometimes too eager. Ho in «gung ho» is part of a phrase, so the whole phrase has its own meaning.
fact: Ho is an interjection: a short word that shouts or bursts out, like «Land ho!».
fact: «Land ho!» means «land ahead!» and sailors shout it when they see land.
fact: «Ho ho ho!» is the laugh of Father Christmas, also called Santa.
fact: «Westward ho!» means «go west!».
fact: Some slang uses of ho are a very rude insult. Never use them; they are not acceptable at school.
exercise: What does «Land ho!» mean? | Hello, my friend | Stop the boat now | Lunch is ready | *Land ahead!
exercise: Santa laughs «Ho ho ho!». What is that sound? | *A laugh | A shout of warning | A question | A sneeze
exercise: «Gung ho» came into English from a Chinese slogan. Now it means | *very eager | very sleepy | very sad | very quiet
test: What does «Westward ho!» mean? | Stop here! | Sail home! | *Go west! | Go north!
test: Why can the same word ho mean different things? | Because it is spelled in different ways | *Because the words around it give clues | Because it rhymes with go | Because it is only ever a noun
exam: Ho, in «Land ho!» and «Ho ho ho!», is which kind of word? | Noun | Verb | *Interjection | Adjective
